00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:13.060
Good morning, and welcome to the second day
of Next.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:16.910
And today I’m excited to announce that we
are adding three more regions to the set.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:21.670
I hope you like live demos, because this is
the first of four live GCP demos you’re

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.930
going to see in the keynote today.

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:26.689
And I couldn’t be more excited to have Spanner
be the first one.

00:00:26.689 --> 00:00:32.349
Starting today, GCP VMs will come with up
to 64 Cores and 416 GB of memory.

00:00:32.349 --> 00:00:37.850
First, I’d like to congratulate Google on
being the absolute first to have cloud services

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:39.600
on our next generation Xeon processors, Skylake.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.600
Great, thank you.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:48.940
For a one- or three-year commitment, you receive
a discount for up to 57 percent, billed monthly

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.040
with no upfront payment.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:56.360
You can add a GPU to any of VM configuration
and start it up in under a minute, much faster

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.570
than our competitors.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:03.260
Within a matter of minutes, we are able to
mix the CPU’s GPUs high ratio, memory close

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:08.790
to the processor, the way we need for particular
algorithms to work in a very good way.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:13.810
So, this is a big advantage that we get out
of Google, but I would say that another advantage,

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:17.650
as Urs Hölzle said himself, I think Google
should be very proud of having someone like

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:23.190
this...Alright, let that be stricken from
the record, that was not in the script.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:31.030
We put a security chip on all our new machines
to serve as the basis of trust for that machine’s

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:32.030
identity.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:34.590
This is a custom chip, designed by Google.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:41.100
We know exactly what it does, and it helps
us protect serves against tampering even at

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.560
the BIOS level.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:48.880
The chip, in fact, is so small that I’m
actually wearing one on my earring here.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:49.880
3

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.640
We’re announcing several new security features
that we’re adding to GCP and G Suite.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.130
Data loss prevention...DLP makes it easy.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:03.450
When he ends the chat we will use data loss
to identify and redact sensitive information.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:08.840
This isn’t just dumb OCR, you’ll notice
it didn’t block out the expiration date

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:12.900
or the name, because we told the API that
the only information we wanted to eliminate

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.680
is credit card numbers.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:19.640
Next, Cloud’s key management service is
now generally available, and we also added

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.310
tools that let you safely access that secure
content.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.480
The first one is Identity Aware Proxy security
content.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.600
And last but not least Security Key Enforcement
is now available.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:36.000
It’s really important to us that for developers
and Windows developers, that we meet them

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.750
exactly where they are.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:41.819
We want to be a great windows platform, perhaps
the best Windows platform.

00:02:41.819 --> 00:02:46.870
Today, we’re announcing the general availability
of SQL Server Enterprise and that actually

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:49.820
includes support for a higher availability
as well as clustering.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:56.230
Also, a beta of .NET and that will be available
for both App Engine and Container Engine.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:59.900
And to help people on this journey, we’re
announcing a new Windows partner program so

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:08.090
now Linux or Windows, MySQL, Oracle or SQL
Server, and as of today, also a beta of Managed

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:09.090
PostgreSQL.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:13.810
We recently added, right from the cloud console,
the ability to migrate a virtual machine to

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:15.020
GCP.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:18.420
And this is more than transcoding an offline
image.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.300
It is actually the live migration of a running
server to GCP.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:26.680
So what is the best test for moving to the
cloud with minimal friction?

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:33.440
Probably to be able to do that with zero downtime,
so welcome Jack Constantine from LUSH to tell

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:34.630
us about their journey.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:40.989
The actual migration was from December the
1st until December the 22nd, so it's not just

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:44.220
a little bit business critical, we’re talking
peak trade time.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.940
It was a huge deal.

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:52.310
We moved 17 websites, from all over the world
with customer data, order data, product data.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.900
You obviously want that all up and stable.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:59.220
You know, back in 2008 Google App Engine was
really it was pioneering serverless, and it

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.220
was ahead of its time.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:06.560
So now out of the box, we support seven popular
languages - Java, Ruby, Go, PHP, Python, C#

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:12.050
and Node.js - or you can now for the first
time, bring your own run time, bring your

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:14.680
own framer, to App Engine Flexible Environments.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.190
As long as it runs in a Docker container,
it runs on App Engine.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:23.960
Serverless is a really important concept and
today we’re announcing the beta of our newest

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.629
serverless offering, Cloud Functions.

00:04:25.629 --> 00:04:30.409
We’ve also integrated the Cloud Functions
that we just talked about with Firebase.

00:04:30.409 --> 00:04:35.819
That allows Firebase developers to extend
their backend logic in crazy ways and today

00:04:35.819 --> 00:04:38.569
we are making firebase even more powerful.

00:04:38.569 --> 00:04:44.150
Now you can access any GCS bucket, anywhere
in the world, right from Firebase.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:48.629
We integrated cloud storage into the Firebase
STKs.

00:04:48.629 --> 00:04:54.580
Great companies use data to react faster to
the market, build novel new products and even

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.909
change culturally how they work.

00:04:56.909 --> 00:05:00.300
Announcing the Google BigQuery Data Transfer
Service.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:06.699
What that does is it automates the transfer
of data from SaaS applications in the BigQuery,

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:09.050
but it does it on a scheduled, managed basis.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:12.830
That’s why today we are introducing Cloud
Dataprep.

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:18.240
Dataprep is this intelligent service that
allows you to visually explore and clean your

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.990
data so that is can be integrated into a BigQuery
environment.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:25.389
As Apigee, we know a thing or two about APIs.

00:05:25.389 --> 00:05:28.249
We process billions of API codes in our cloud.

00:05:28.249 --> 00:05:31.949
You process billions of API codes using our
technology, in your cloud.

00:05:31.949 --> 00:05:34.530
Everybody is choosing the cloud.

